## Proposed Solution for Issue #13676

### Root Cause Analysis & Fix
The POST /api/users endpoint forwards the raw request body to the persistence layer, allowing arbitrary fields (including a plaintext password) to be stored, which creates malformed accounts and leaks credentials. Introducing a Zod schema that whitelists only the permitted fields (email, fullName, optional bio and role), strips any password field, and returns a 400 response on validation failure fixes the issue.

### Proposed Code Patch
```
--- a/src/routes/users.ts
+++ b/src/routes/users.ts
@@
-import { Router } from 'express';
-import { createUser, getAllUsers } from '../services/userService';
+import { Router } from 'express';
+import { createUser, getAllUsers } from '../services/userService';
+import { z } from 'zod';
@@
-const router = Router();
+const router = Router();
+
+// Zod schema for user creation payload
+const createUserSchema = z.object({
+  email: z.string().email(),
+  fullName: z.string().min(1),
+  bio: z.string().optional(),
+  role: z.enum(['user', 'admin']).optional(),
+}).strict(); // reject unknown keys
@@
-router.post('/', async (req, res) => {
-  const user = await createUser(req.body);
-  res.status(201).json(user);
-});
+router.post('/', async (req, res) => {
+  // Validate request payload
+  const parseResult = createUserSchema.safeParse(req.body);
+  if (!parseResult.success) {
+    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid user payload', details: parseResult.error.errors });
+  }
+
+  // Ensure no plaintext password is persisted even if sent accidentally
+  const sanitizedPayload = parseResult.data;
+
+  const user = await createUser(sanitizedPayload);
+  res.status(201).json(user);
+});
*** End of File ***
```

